WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.200
Is "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever" a&nbsp;
worthy follow-up to the original film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.140
or does it fall short of excellence?&nbsp;
Here's what the critics are saying.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:11.520
It's premiere week for "Black Panther: Wakanda&nbsp;
Forever," and members of the press have attended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.180
special screenings of the film. Before they're&nbsp;
allowed to give their official reviews, writers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.260
critics, and influencers take to social media&nbsp;
to share their initial reactions to the project.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.280
Now that critics can expand on those initial&nbsp;
reactions, we're getting down into the nitty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.420
gritty of "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever."&nbsp;
There are still some rave reviews for the flick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.173
but some more negative responses&nbsp;
are starting to come through.

00:00:30.173 --> 00:00:33.420
As of Tuesday, November 8, the Rotten&nbsp;
Tomatoes score for "Black Panther:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:37.860
Wakanda Forever" has been hovering in the&nbsp;
mid-90s. As these reviews continue to pile in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:42.240
this number will rise and fall but it looks&nbsp;
like critics are absolutely loving the sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.220
but it's not without its flaws. As to be expected,&nbsp;
many reviews mention the emotional roller coaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:51.300
fans will ride while watching "Wakanda Forever."&nbsp;
Deadline's Pete Hammond said of the sequel,

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.420
"There can be no doubt Coogler has&nbsp;
meticulously thought all of this out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:58.140
while still delivering the rip roaring&nbsp;
action sequences and uncredited surprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.800
appearances we expect from any Marvel&nbsp;
movie, but especially its crown jewel."

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:06.180
Coogler was praised in many reviews, which should&nbsp;
come as quite a relief for the director who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.020
a tremendous amount of pressure on his shoulders&nbsp;
when making this film. According to Richard Roeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.520
of the Chicago Sun-Times, "'Black Panther: Wakanda&nbsp;
Forever," quote, "succeeds as a new adventure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.360
and as a tribute to a wonderful actor who&nbsp;
was taken from this world decades too soon."

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.680
Additionally, Jamie Broadnax of Black Girl&nbsp;
Nerds is calling "Wakanda Forever" the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:27.060
film of Phase 4, making it the perfect film to&nbsp;
close out this segment of "The Multiverse Saga."

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.820
But while many critics are praising&nbsp;
the movie, a nice chunk of them have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:33.900
some quibbles with the movie's pacing and&nbsp;
focus. Richard Lawson of Vanity Fair writes,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.905
"Some will no doubt miss the tight&nbsp;
focus and energy of Black Panther.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.905 --> 00:01:40.440
This sequel is more scattered, a vast&nbsp;
expansion with a hole at its center."

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.700
This is a common theme among critics, as many&nbsp;
suggest it doesn't stack up to its predecessor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.780
To be fair, "Black Panther" is often hailed as&nbsp;
one of the best superhero movies of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.572
so it's not an insult to say&nbsp;
its sequel doesn't match it.

00:01:51.572 --> 00:01:54.960
Robbie Collin of The Daily Telegraph&nbsp;
held nothing back, saying the film is

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.640
"A near-three-hour endurance run of gloomy&nbsp;
photography and turgidly staged, emotionally empty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.200
two-way conversations, all seemingly designed&nbsp;
to sap cast and viewers' combined will to live."

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.480
Matt Singer of Screen Crush&nbsp;
calls the movie, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.080
"A messy sprawl of an ensemble piece"&nbsp;
and says in his review that watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.780
the original before seeing the sequel is&nbsp;
a bad move because it pales in comparison.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:13.800
"Is this your king?"

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.060
Rolling Stone's K. Austin Collins offered&nbsp;
a balanced critique in his review, writing,

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:21.240
"The movie isn't always on such sure&nbsp;
footing. But that's almost appropriate:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.556
a messier movie trying to reckon&nbsp;
with a messier range of feelings."

00:02:24.556 --> 00:02:28.800
"When you left without saying a word, it hurt."

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.240
Additionally, some critics mentioned the movie is&nbsp;
just a little bit too long, like Owen Gleiberman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:37.740
of Variety suggesting that 20 minutes could&nbsp;
have been shaved off of its 161-minute runtime.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:42.480
Overall, "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever" appears&nbsp;
to be a great addition to the ever-expanding MCU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.920
Where it falls in the ranks is entirely up to&nbsp;
you, but if these reviews are any indication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.640
it's definitely in the top half.&nbsp;
Brian Truitt of USA Today wrote,

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.880
"Director Ryan Coogler delivers a&nbsp;
powerful follow-up to the phenomenal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:58.560
2018 Black Panther that's funny, clever and&nbsp;
heartbreaking, impressive in its world-building,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.000
honest in its view of world politics and&nbsp;
naturally packed with huge action sequences."

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.120
It definitely seems like it'll help&nbsp;
those suffering from Marvel fatigue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.560
after the less-than-stellar Phase 4 films like&nbsp;
"Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.440
"Eternals," and "Black Widow."&nbsp;
Ross Bonamine of Collider added,

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.980
"Wakanda Forever isn't perfect, but its&nbsp;
ability to handle this tightrope walk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:21.000
between exploration of loss and a larger&nbsp;
superhero film makes this one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.560
moving MCU entries so far, and one of the best&nbsp;
films to come out of Marvel's Phase Four."

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:29.520
According to critics, it sounds like Coogler&nbsp;
has delivered a slightly messy but compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.400
sequel to the original film.&nbsp;
Now all that's left is to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.121
what the fans think when "Wakanda Forever"&nbsp;
officially premieres on November 11, 2022.

